  year={2025},
  volume={},
  number={},
  pages={1-6},
  abstract={This paper proposes a human-centred pedagogical framework for integrating Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) into Digital Humanities (DH) education. The model consists of three phases: (1) Natural Language (NL) reasoning with Python; (2) object-oriented modeling; and (3) prompt-based code generation using Large Language Models (LLMs). Students translate scholarly questions iteratively into executable code, treating LLMs as fallible collaborators whose outputs require critical supervision. Having been piloted in a Master’s-level DH course, this approach fosters computational, AI and ethical literacies, particularly among students with limited coding experience. The model is modular and adaptable, supporting reflective and interdisciplinary learning across diverse educational settings, including those with limited resources. As AI reshapes the production of knowledge, this linguistically grounded model provides a scalable basis for Cyber Humanities pedagogy.},
